Simulator components visible in assets/demo.gif

The one binary this repository ships is footage of the author's own runs, so it contains rendered images of robot
models, scene assets and textures that come from the simulator stack rather than from here. Named for that reason:

- robosuite, https://github.com/ARISE-Initiative/robosuite, version 1.4.0 as installed.
  Copyright (c) 2022 Stanford Vision and Learning Lab and UT Robot Perception and Learning Lab.
  Licensed under the MIT License, reproduced in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/robosuite-MIT.txt.
  The text was taken out of the installed distribution rather than from the repository page, with its line
  endings normalised to LF as every text file here is and nothing else altered. That source is worth stating
  because the packaging metadata of the same distribution records the licence field
  itself as UNKNOWN, which is a field its packaging never filled in rather than a statement about terms: it also
  records LICENSE and AUTHORS as licence files, and both are present. Reading the field and stopping would have
  produced the wrong answer, and this file gave that wrong answer until the distribution was opened.
  The file reproduced here carries one further notice of its own, that robosuite contains a partial implementation
  of DeepMind MuJoCo under the Apache License, Version 2.0. It is retained by shipping the file whole.
- MuJoCo, version 3.7.0 as installed, which is the renderer that produced the frames.
  Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0, declared as License-Expression: Apache-2.0 by the installed
  distribution. No separate copy is shipped because the licence text that distribution carries is byte identical
  to the LICENSE file at the root of this repository, which was established by comparing their hashes rather than
  by assuming that two Apache copies agree. That distribution also ships its own third party inventory,
  LICENSES_THIRD_PARTY.md, for anyone tracing the contents of a rendered scene further upstream.
- DejaVu Sans Bold, the typeface the overlay captions are set in. The frames carry rendered glyphs, not the font
  file, and nothing here redistributes the font itself. Named because the section is an inventory of what is in
  the image and text is in the image. Established rather than guessed: the figure was drawn with matplotlib at
  its default sans serif family and bold weight, which resolves to the DejaVuSans-Bold.ttf that matplotlib ships.
  Its terms are in that distribution's LICENSE_DEJAVU, the Bitstream Vera Fonts licence, Copyright (c) 2003
  Bitstream, Inc., with the DejaVu modifications placed in the public domain and imported Arev glyphs
  Copyright (c) Tavmjong Bah.
- matplotlib, version 3.10.9 as installed, which drew the overlay half of the frames as MuJoCo rendered the
  scene half. Licensed under its own terms, titled "License agreement for matplotlib versions 1.3.0 and later",
  which the installed distribution declares as its licence and ships in full. Named here for the same reason
  MuJoCo is: this section inventories what put pixels into the shipped image, and a tool named without terms is
  the omission this file elsewhere refuses to make.
- LIBERO, whose entry above carries its terms and whose MIT text is in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/LIBERO-MIT.txt. It
  supplies most of what is actually visible: the task scenes and their backgrounds, the manipulated objects and
  the surface textures. robosuite supplies the robot. Listing the renderer and the robot while leaving out the
  source of everything they render would have been the wrong division, and it stood in this file until the frames
  were opened and the objects in them traced back to the asset tree they come from. That tree names three
  families of externally sourced geometry in its own directory layout, turbosquid_objects, stable_hope_objects
  and stable_scanned_objects, and objects from all three appear in the shipped frames. LIBERO declares terms for
  its codebase and its datasets and says nothing separately about those families, so the entry above is the whole
  of what its own declaration covers, and a reader who needs to go further upstream than that should start there
  rather than here.

What is still open is recorded as open, which is the honest state: no view is taken here on whether a rendering of
a scene is a derivative work of the assets in it. The licences above are named because they can be, not because
that question has been answered. Nothing from any of the three is redistributed as source.

